.SH NAME
tree_row \- See the module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
TreeRow::new(title: impl Into<String>)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
See the module.
.PP
\fBTreeRow\fR: a row that can hold other rows, or any elements, nested under it. Inspired by \fBExpanderRow\fR, made for hierarchies: the \fBarrow\fR at the start opens and closes it (GTK's \fBGtkTreeExpander\fR), the \fBtitle\fR is a separate target that activates the row (select it, open it), and a leaf keeps the arrow's width so labels line up.
.PP
\fBScope.\fR A \fBTreeRow\fR nests elements *within a page*: a group of settings, an outline, a file list, rows that belong together on one screen. It is \fBnot for logic that spans pages\fR: moving between pages, a sidebar of destinations or a navigation stack belong to \fBNavigationView\fR and the sidebar components, which the HIG keeps flat. If opening a row changes what page you are on, it is navigation, not a tree row.
.PP
Children are anything: more \fBTreeRow\fRs, \fBActionRow\fRs, switch rows, or a plain widget. They are pushed in and shown on the shaded list that slides open, each indented by \fBindent\fR (which starts at the toolkit's \fB[rows] expander\-indent\fR and is overridden per row).
.PP
.nf
.RS
TreeRow::new("Network")
    .subtitle("Proxy and name servers")
    .expanded(self.open)
    .on_toggle(Msg::Network)
    .push(TreeRow::new("Proxy").subtitle("Automatic").on_activate(Msg::Proxy))
    .push(SwitchRow::new("Use VPN", self.vpn, Msg::Vpn))
    .push(my_custom_widget())
.RE
.fi
.PP
Controlled: the app owns \fBexpanded\fR and \fBselected\fR. A row with children shows the arrow; \fBexpandable\fR shows it before the children exist (lazy loading).
